{"id":"b5a94da0-e163-481d-959e-f1c91c1c7afe","object":"chat.completion","created":1793336533,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Reading remaining logger.py and the package init to wire imports safely.\n\n```python\nr1 = read_range(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", 90, 130)\nr2 = read_file(\"deepinfer/__init__.py\")\nprint(r1[\"content\"])\nprint(\"=== init ===\")\nprint(r2[\"content\"])\n```","reasoning_content":"I need to implement changes. Let me plan the edits.\n\nenviron.py: add config item `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS` with factory method returning None if unset, else int >= 32 with error text containing variable name. Note EnvVar.__get__ wraps exception with f\"{self.name}: {e}\" so message already contains name. But the required message must include variable name — the wrapper adds it. But if we raise inside init ValueError(\"...\"), wrapper wraps. Actually for a non-cached first access error the wrapper raises type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\"). Fine. But to be safe, include name in our own message too? The test may call the descriptor directly... They'd likely test error message contains variable name when accessing envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. Either way works; I'll include explicit name text to be safe? Careful: wording \"错误文字须包含变量名\" — include in our own message is safest.\n\nAdd factory method `int_or_none_min(min_value)` maybe. Let me add:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, min_value: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < min_value:\n                raise ValueError(f\"must be >= {min_value}, got {value}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nThen in envs Logging section:\n\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(min_value=32)\n```\n\nWait, error must contain the variable name. The wrapper in __get__ does `f\"{self.name}: {e}\"`. So accessing envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS gives \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'abc'\". Good. But if the test catches error from the descriptor... Fine.\n\nHowever note: empty string \"\" → int(\"\") raises ValueError with message not containing name from our own init; wrapper adds it. Ok.\n\nlogger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with `max_message_chars` keyword-only param. Validate: if not None: must be int (not bool) and >=32, else ValueError.\n\nWhere to truncate? In format(): need to compute record.message after args substitution but before prefixing. Approach: use `record.getMessage()` to compute full message, then if too long, we need to replace prefix+message? Simpler: build the format string with placeholder? The spec: budget applies only to message body, not prefix, exception traceback or stack_info. Must not modify shared record's msg/args/exc_info/stack_info.\n\nImplementation: compute `message = record.getMessage()`. Then if truncation needed, we need super().format(record) which uses record.getMessage() internally... We can temporarily set record.message? super().format sets record.message = record.getMessage() and caches. Actually logging.Formatter.format does:\n\n```python\nrecord.message = record.getMessage()\nif self.usesTime(): record.asctime = ...\ns = self.formatMessage(record)\nif record.exc_info: ...\nif record.stack_info: ...\n```\n\nIt mutates record.message (an attribute on record). The spec forbids rewriting msg, args, exc_info, stack_info — record.message is derived cache, but mutating it may affect other handlers: subsequent handler's format() recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() anyway. So it's safe-ish, but risky. Better: format with a temporary shallow copy? Copying LogRecord via copy module... `logging.LogRecord` can be copied with `copy.copy(record)`. But allowed imports? copy isn't in the allowed import list for action code, but the source file can import anything (it's business source). Hmm, but minimal is better.\n\nAlternative approach that's clean: implement custom formatMessage using truncated message:\n\nOverride format to:\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    truncated = ...compute message string truncated...\n```\nBut super().format uses record's own message. We can do:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    budget = self.max_message_chars\n    if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = _truncate(message, budget)\n    # produce output using the truncated message without touching record attributes\n    ...\n```\nThen need to format prefix with truncated message. Use self._fmt? Instead: create a lightweight proxy? We could compute the formatted prefix from `self._style` — but style's format expects record.message. Hmm.\n\nOption: use a shallow copy of the record where msg=truncated message, args=():\n\n```python\nimport copy\nrecord = copy.copy(record)\nrecord.msg = message\nrecord.args = ()\n```\nBut that changes the copy only, not the original. That's acceptable: \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg...\" — we don't modify the original. That's the cleanest way. Note exc_info, stack_info preserved by copy (copy of the record includes exc_text cache — careful: record.exc_text may be cached from previous handler; copying keeps it, fine).\n\nActually careful with copy.copy of a LogRecord: it has __dict__, shallow copy works fine.\n\nBut computing record.getMessage() then setting msg to truncated message with args=() means format's record.getMessage() returns truncated message. Good.\n\nThen continue with super().format(record) on the copy, and multiline prefixing logic uses `record.message` from copy — fine.\n\nAlso `keep_full_message` attribute comes through copy.\n\nImplement:\n\n```python\n_MAX_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return message\n    marker_len = len(_MAX_OMITTED_MARKER)\n    remaining = max_chars - marker_len\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return message[:max_chars]\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + _MAX_OMITTED_MARKER + (message[-tail:] if tail > 0 else \"\")\n```\n\nCheck example: budget 32, marker 15 → remaining 17, head=9, tail=8. ✓ \"保留开头向上取整的一半\" → ceil(17/2)=9, tail 8. ✓\n\nValidation helper:\n\n```python\ndef _validate_max_message_chars(value):\n    if value is None:\n        return None\n    if isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, int) or value < 32:\n        raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {value!r}\")\n    return value\n```\n\nInit: `def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None)`.\n\nIn format:\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    keep_full = getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and not keep_full:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n        if truncated != message:\n            record = copy.copy(record)\n            record.msg = truncated\n            record.args = ()\n            record.message = truncated  # hmm, format sets it anyway\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\nNote: super().format(record) sets record.message = record.getMessage() on the copy.\n\nBut wait — multi-line prefixing path uses `record.message`; fine.\n\nAlso exc_text: the copy has exc_text None initially typically.\n\nNow _setup_logger: pass max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. Need import: `from deepinfer import envs`? Check logger.py imports — check whether environ imported. Need to read remainder of logger.py to see rest (maybe there's `from deepinfer.environ import envs`